Query Optimization Basics

.Poorly written queries:
    .make DB slow
    .use too many resources
    .cause locking problems
    .give users bad experience 



.Best Practices:

1. Use indexes wisely 
    .indexes help DB find data faster 
        .primary index - automatically created on the primary key; keeps values unique and fast to access
        .secondary index - created manually on non-primary key columns to improve query performance
        .clustered index - determines the arrangement of data in a table
                    .in some tables, primary key is automatically indexed 
        .non-clustered index - contains pointers to the data 
                    .multiple exist per table 

        
    Indexing guidelines:
        .index columns used often in WHERE, JOIN, or ORDER BY clauses 
        .avoid too many indexes - they slow down INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE
        .check and monitor index usage regularly to keep queries fast 


2. Avoid select *: Choose only required columns
    .makes queries slow, esp for large tables or when joining multiple tables 
    .avoiding will:
        .use less memory and run faster
        .let DB skip unneeded columns 
        .make queries simpler and easier to read 


3. Limit rows with WHERE and LIMIT 
    .too many rows make queries slow
    .use WHERE to filter data, and LIMIT to get only the rows needed 
    .benefits:
        .makes queries faster 
        .avoids overload - sends only the needed data 
        .useful for testing and previewing results 

    
4. Write efficient WHERE clauses 
    .using functions or calculations on columns can stop the DB from using indexes 
        .don't use functions on columns 
        .avoid calculations on columns 
        .write conditions so indexes can be used efficiently 



5. Use Joins smartly 
    .join only the tables needed, and filter data before joining
    .use INNER join instead of OUTER join if unmatched rows are not needed 
    .benefits:
        .faster join processing
        .avoids unwanted Cartesian products 
        .helps DB choose an efficient execution plan 


6. Avoid N+1 query problems 
    .N+1 happens when I run a query to get a list, then run extra queries for each item 
        .instead, fetch related data in a single query using JOINS instead 
    .benefits:
        .fewer DB cells
        .faster response time
        .reduces load on DB 


7. Use EXISTS instead of IN - for subqueries 
    .when checking whether a specific record exists in a table 
        .EXISTS stops searching ASAP finding the first matching record: IN processes all the results before making the comparison 
    .benefits:
        .less memory used for large subqueries 


8. Avoid wildcards at the start of LIKE
    .don't start a LIKE pattern with % because it disables index use and causes a full table scan 
    .benefits:
        .reduces scanning overhead
        .keeps searches fast and index-friendly 


9. Use query execution plan 
    .
    .benefits:
        .helps identify full table scans
        .reveals if indexes are used 
        .guides optimization decisions 


10. Use UNION ALL instead of UNION 
    .UNION removes duplicates - adds sorting overhead 
    .UNION ALL if duplicates don't matter 
    .benefits:
        .avoids unnecessary sorting
        .merges results faster
        .better for large datasets 


